<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saichō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">767–<a href="/years/822">822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Dengyō Daishi, Hirono</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saichō was a Japanese Buddhist monk credited with founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> school, posthumously titled Dengyō Daishi. Fully ordained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a>, he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, where his community became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>, and in 804 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> to study the Tiantai school. On his return he made Enryaku-ji the centre of Tendai, joining Tiantai meditation, study, precepts and ritual with esoteric practices. He sought an ordination based on the bodhisattva precepts rather than the traditional Vinaya; the Buddhist establishment protested, and his request was granted only seven days after his death in 822. His school laid the ground for later Pure Land and Zen traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saicho.jpg" alt="Saichō" width="330" height="544" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:最澄像_一乗寺蔵_平安時代.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tendai, brought from China by the monk <b>Saichō</b>, rested on the Lotus Sutra and taught that enlightenment was open to “every creature”; its monastery on Mount Hiei, above the capital, grew close to the court and taught great reverence for the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
